system "ISO:prs-Arab:Latn:233-3-2026" {

metadata {
  authority_id iso
  id 233-3-2026
  language iso-639-3:prs
  source_script Arab
  destination_script Latn
  name "ISO 233-3:2026 Information and documentation -- Transliteration of Perso-Arabic characters into Latin characters -- Part 3: Persian language"
  url https://www.iso.org/committee/48820.html
  # Source document: interscript-private-references/iso/ISO-233-3-2026.pdf
  # (purchased published edition, ISO Store order OP-1130631, downloaded
  # 2026-11-19; verification-grade provenance).
  creation_date 2026
  adoption_date 2026
  description {    Third edition (cancels and replaces ISO 233-3:2023 as a minor
    revision; the corpus's legacy map implements the 1999 first edition).
    Encodes the strict transliteration of clause 4, the one-to-one
    fully reversible system: Table 1 consonants (32 rows), Table 2
    vowels (alef madda and the three written diacritical vowel signs),
    Table 3 conventional signs (tashdid and the three tanvin marks),
    4.4.3 ta marbuta per ISO 233, 4.4.4 the Allah word, Table 4 hamze
    forms, Table 5 the ezafe he-plus-hamze form, Table 1 note d's
    strict provision for the yeh-plus-superscript-alef clarity variant,
    Table 6 punctuation and Table 7 numerals. The clause 5 modified
    transliteration (Tables 8-11) is not reversible - it depends on
    word position and on whether a letter functions as consonant or
    vowel - and is not encoded.
  }

  notes {
    note "Encoded from the purchased published edition (ISO Store order OP-1130631, downloaded 2026-11-19); the PDF text layer is clean and the hex column is authoritative over the printed glyphs. Extracted 2026-11-19."
    note "NFC quirk preserved exactly as the published standard prints it: the zal row (ذ, hex 1E95) and the zain row (ظ, hex 1E93) use precomposed codepoints, while the thal row (ث, hex 0073+0331) and the dad row (ض, hex 007A+0324) are printed decomposed; no precomposed forms exist for those two. Note that the decomposed zal (U+007A+U+0331) is canonically equivalent to the precomposed U+1E95, so NFC input affects which of the two Arabic letters a normalized application resolves; 4.1.3 requires systems to treat precomposed and decomposed characters equally on input."
    note "Clause 4.1.3: a system transliterating from Latin into Persian script shall be case insensitive, and precomposed/decomposed input must be treated equally; both are input-normalization concerns left to the implementing engine, not rows of the map."
    note "Tolerance notes of the source, flagged and not encoded as rows: Table 1 note b (U+2018 may be encountered for the ayn), note c (Arabic yeh U+064A may be encountered in existing text; no strict value is given for it, so bare U+064A passes through unromanized), Table 1 note d and Table 5 note b (U+06C0 may be encountered; it is not canonically equivalent to the encoded he-plus-hamze sequence U+0647+U+0654 and is not permitted in ISIRI 6219), Table 4 note a (U+2019 may be encountered for the hamze apostrophe). Bare alef maqsur U+0649 likewise has no explicit strict row: the source says Persian usually writes it as U+06CC or, for clarity, U+06CC+U+0670 (the latter is the encoded ý row)."
    note "Strict alef rule (Table 1 note a and 4.2.1): alef is always transliterated as a-macron, even as the bearer of a written short vowel (alef + pish = a-macron + o, e.g. اُمید = āomyd); this falls out of the character-by-character rows."
    note "The Allah word (4.4.4, hex 0627 0644 0644 0651 0670 0647) is encoded as one strict multi-character row producing Allāh (capital A as printed); the superscript alef U+0670 occurs in no other row. Reverse-direction limitation, flagged: the transliteration engine inverts rows with a single-character source or target, and this row is multi-character on both sides, so a reversed run does not reconstruct the six-character source from Allāh (the capital A, the shadda and the superscript alef are the unrecoverable parts). The forward direction - the bibliographic direction of the standard - is fully faithful."
    note "Ezafe in the strict system (4.6): the written zir-e ezafe is treated as zir (e) by the character rows; he plus hamze above (hex 0647+0654) is the encoded h-apostrophe row (Table 5) as a pair row; after final alef or vav the ezafe ye is plain yeh (y) per 4.6.2. The clause 5 modified system's -e/-ye hyphenation and the tashdid double-letter U+035F convention are part of the non-reversible system and are not encoded."
    note "Forward collision inherited from the source, flagged: he followed by the standalone hamza letter (hex 0647, 0621, Table 4 row 1 + Table 1 row 31) and he with hamze above (hex 0647+0654, Table 5) both romanize h-apostrophe, and the document provides no disambiguator. The reverse direction resolves h-apostrophe to the standalone form (he plus hamza) because the ezafe pair row does not invert; the choice is recorded here rather than hacked around."
    note "Clause 4.4.1: an Arabic element present in Persian text but not covered by this document follows ISO 233-1; no ISO 233-1 rows are added here."
    note "Provenance lineage: the legacy corpus map iso-prs-Arab-Latn-233-3-1999.isc implements the 1999 first edition (a simplified system); neither the 1999 nor the 2023 second edition document is held, and the legacy map is untouched. This map is the 2026 third edition strict system."
  }
}

tests {
   "آذَر" ->  "âẕar"
   "آذر" ->  "âẕr"
   "سَم" ->  "sam"
   "سم" ->  "sm"
   "پُر" ->  "por"
   "پر" ->  "pr"
   "سِپَر" ->  "separ"
   "سپر" ->  "spr"
   "اُمید" ->  "āomyd"
   "امید" ->  "āmyd"
   "مثلاً" ->  "ms̱lāã"
   "مربّع" ->  "mrbʺʻ"
   "جزء" ->  "jzʼ"
   "رأس" ->  "rāʼs"
   "سؤال" ->  "svʼāl"
   "پائین" ->  "pāyʼyn"
   "اللّٰه" ->  "Allāh"
   "هٔ" ->  "hʼ"
   "یٰ" ->  "ý"
   "ة" ->  "ẗ"
   "،" ->  ","
   "؛" ->  ";"
   "؟" ->  "?"
   "۱۲۳" ->  "123"
# Exhaustive per-row tests for iso-prs-Arab-Latn-233-3-2026, generated from the source
# document's table.
# Source: ISO 233-3:2026(en), third edition, purchased published edition
# (ISO Store order OP-1130631, downloaded 2026-11-19; verification-grade
# provenance). STRICT transliteration only (clause 4, the fully reversible
# system); the clause 5 modified transliteration is not reversible and is
# not encoded. Rows in document order: Table 1 rows 1-32 (consonants; NFC
# quirk preserved as printed: the zal row uses precomposed U+1E95 and the
# zain row precomposed U+1E93, while the sin-e-thalathah row is the
# decomposed U+0073+U+0331 and the dad row the decomposed U+007A+U+0324 - no
# precomposed forms exist for these two), Table 2 rows 1-4 (vowels:
# alef-madda as a-circumflex, zebar a, pish o, zir e), Table 3 (tashdid
# double-prime U+02BA and the three tanvin rows), 4.4.3 (ta marbuta to
# t-with-line-below U+1E97 per ISO 233), 4.4.4 (the Allah word as one strict
# row over hex 0627,0644,0644,0651,0670,0647), Table 4 rows 1-4 (hamze
# forms; apostrophe U+02BC), Table 5 row 1 (ezafe he plus hamze above h with
# following U+02BC), Table 1 note d strict provision (yeh plus superscript
# alef clarity variant to y-acute U+00FD), Table 6 (three punctuation
# marks), Table 7 (ten numerals).
# Provenance artifact: scripts/row-sources/iso-prs-Arab-Latn-233-3-2026.json (61 rows).
# generated-by: maps/scripts/gen-row-tests.mjs (2026-11-19); regenerate from the artifact, do not hand-edit.
  "ا" -> "ā"
  "ب" -> "b"
  "پ" -> "p"
  "ت" -> "t"
  "ث" -> "s̱"
  "ج" -> "j"
  "چ" -> "č"
